<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Swedish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sweden is a kingdom in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on the eastern side of the Scandinavian Peninsula. In the seventeenth century it became the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>, a Baltic great power whose armies intervened decisively in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and fought repeatedly against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Its defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s Russia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> ended its imperial era and helped launch the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sweden.jpg" alt="Sweden" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Harald Søby and others. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sweden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khmelnytsky's multi-vectored diplomacy, weighing an Orthodox–Protestant bloc, a third partner's place in the Commonwealth or the Ottoman orbit, drew the Tsardom, Poland, <b>Sweden</b>, Transylvania and the Porte into the Ukrainian question.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kalmar Union, the union of the three Scandinavian crowns formed in 1397 under Queen Margaret I. In 1523 <b>Sweden</b> broke away under Gustav Vasa, and in the same year the Danish nobility deposed the Union king Christian II and elected his uncle, Duke Frederick of Schleswig-Holstein, as Frederick…</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A Griffin prince, Eric of Pomerania, became king of Denmark, Norway and <b>Sweden</b> in 1397, and later ended his days as a minor duke in Pomerania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War John George I first stayed neutral, then joined Gustavus Adolphus of <b>Sweden</b>; the Saxon contingent fled at the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate Peace of Prague with the emperor in 1635.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The greatest threat came from <b>Sweden</b>, which received the secularised archbishopric as the Duchy of Bremen-Verden in 1648 and claimed the city as part of it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1569 the grand duchy kept its own army under a grand hetman, and in the seventeenth century its forces fought <b>Sweden</b>, Russia and the Cossacks, suffering a Russian occupation of Vilnius from 1655 to 1661.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li></ul></section>
